\begin{abstract}
Zero-shot action recognition (ZSAR) requires recognizing unseen action categories from videos. CLIP transfers well to images, but naively finetuning it for video underperforms because it lacks mechanisms for temporal dynamics, and existing adaptations either add temporal modules, use simplistic class-level prompts, or enrich only the text side. We present the \emph{Spatiotemporal Dynamic Duo} (STDD), which turns CLIP, an effective spatial expert, into a multi-modal spatiotemporal expert. On the vision side, Space-time Cross Attention (STCA) injects cross-frame dynamics through four parameter-free steps (masking, mixing, padding, shortcutting) without increasing the attention complexity. On the text side, an Action Semantic Knowledge Graph (ASKG) built by prompting an LLM disentangles static appearance from dynamic motion, yielding spatial and temporal prompts that are aligned to video frames with a fine-grained, symmetric frame--prompt objective. With CLIP ViT-L/14, STDD reaches 93.4\% (UCF101, EP1) and 68.7\% (HMDB51, EP1); with ViT-B/16 it obtains 85.2\%, 55.9\% and 75.1\% on UCF101, HMDB51 and Kinetics-600 under EP3, outperforming the compared CLIP-based methods.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Zero-shot action recognition (ZSAR) aims to recognize action categories never seen in training by transferring semantics from seen classes~\cite{jain2015objects2action,wang2017zero,mishra2018generative}. Large vision-language models such as CLIP~\cite{radford2021learning} provide strong visual--text alignment, which makes them natural starting points for ZSAR~\cite{wang2021actionclip,ni2022expanding,weng2023open}. However, directly finetuning CLIP on videos underperforms, because CLIP lacks mechanisms to capture temporal dynamics and nuanced spatiotemporal correlations. This is acute when novel actions differ only in motion: for example, weightlifting variants share a similar visual context but differ in movement patterns, so they cannot be disambiguated from static appearance alone (Figure~\ref{fig:teaser}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/1_teaser}
\caption{Illustration of the challenges without \textit{collaborative multi-modal spatiotemporal understanding}.}
\label{fig:teaser}
\end{figure}

Existing CLIP adaptations fall short in three ways. Some add dedicated temporal modules, increasing model complexity~\cite{yang2023aim,pan2022st,wasim2023vita}; others rely on class-level prompts such as ``a video of \{class\}''~\cite{wang2021actionclip}; and others focus on semantic augmentation with LLMs without improving the temporal modeling on the video side~\cite{chen2024ost,jia2024generating,huang2024froster}. None of them achieves the collaborative multi-modal spatiotemporal understanding that ZSAR requires.

We therefore propose to turn CLIP into a multi-modal spatiotemporal expert by jointly (1) introducing parameter-free temporal dynamics into the vision encoder and (2) enriching semantics with structured spatiotemporal text prompts. Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=*,nosep]
\item A parameter-free \emph{Space-time Cross Attention} (STCA) that augments CLIP's spatial attention with temporal dynamics at no extra attention complexity.
\item An \emph{Action Semantic Knowledge Graph} (ASKG) for structured spatiotemporal text augmentation that disentangles static and dynamic semantics and their relations.
\item A fine-grained, symmetric frame--prompt alignment objective that matches temporal frames to nuanced prompts.
\item State-of-the-art ZSAR on UCF101, HMDB51 and Kinetics-600 under multiple protocols.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}
\paragraph{CLIP for video recognition.} CLIP~\cite{radford2021learning} and related vision-language models~\cite{jia2021scaling,li2022blip,yu2022coca} have been adapted to video through prompt learning and temporal modeling. ActionCLIP~\cite{wang2021actionclip} and X-CLIP~\cite{ni2022expanding} extend CLIP with class-level text and cross-frame communication, Open-VCLIP~\cite{weng2023open} and its extension~\cite{wu2024building} finetune CLIP with regularization for open-vocabulary video recognition, and FROSTER~\cite{huang2024froster} distills frozen CLIP features. Video transformers model temporal relations with factorized or joint space-time attention~\cite{bertasius2021space,arnab2021vivit,liu2022video}, while AIM~\cite{yang2023aim} adapts image models with lightweight adapters. Our STCA, in contrast, adds no parameters and keeps the $O(TN^2)$ cost of spatial attention. The shift operation we build on was used for efficient video modeling in TSM~\cite{lin2019tsm}.

\paragraph{Semantic augmentation for ZSAR.} Early ZSAR work used word embeddings, hand-crafted attributes and elaborative descriptions~\cite{gao2019know,mandal2019out,kim2021daszl,chen2021elaborative}. With large language models~\cite{brown2020language,achiam2023gpt}, recent methods generate richer text: OST~\cite{chen2024ost} uses spatiotemporal descriptors, MAXI~\cite{lin2023match} uses visual captions, AP-CLIP~\cite{jia2024generating} uses action-conditioned prompts, and BIKE~\cite{wu2023bike} and Text4Vis~\cite{wu2023revisiting} exploit attribute and textual knowledge. These works mostly enhance the text side only; STDD couples structured spatial and temporal prompts from a knowledge graph with a vision encoder that models temporal dynamics.

\section{Method: Spatiotemporal Dynamic Duo}
\subsection{Overview}
STDD enhances both modalities in a synergistic way (Figures~\ref{fig:overview} and~\ref{fig:pipeline}). On the vision side, spatial self-attention is converted into space-time cross attention by (1) Window Shift Masking (WSM) to obtain interleaved spatiotemporal tokens, (2) Multi-scale Channel Mixing (MCM) to inject temporal cues across frames, followed by (3) spatial padding and (4) residual shortcutting. On the text side, an ASKG is constructed through LLM prompting to disentangle static appearances and dynamic motions, from which spatial and temporal text prompts are derived. During training, frame-level video features are aligned to prompt-level text features, with auxiliary distillation from frozen CLIP features to preserve generalization.

