\subsection{Space-time Cross Attention}
Given a video $V$ with $T$ frames, each frame is divided into $N$ patches and a [CLS] token is appended per frame. Let $z_t^l$ denote the tokens at block $l$. A standard ViT block computes $z'^l_t=\mathrm{MHSA}(\mathrm{LN}(z_t^{l-1}))+z_t^{l-1}$ and $z_t^l=\mathrm{MLP}(\mathrm{LN}(z'^l_t))+\hat z_t^l$, where $\hat z_t^l$ is obtained by our four-step operation.

\paragraph{Window Shift Masking (WSM).} We define a spatial window of size $w_1\times w_2$ (default $2\times2$) per frame and apply a mask ratio $r$ (default 0.5) to discard tokens at specific positions. The mask shifts over time, so different spatial positions are retained in different frames, creating interleaved spatiotemporal token sets. The mask is generated periodically, $M_t^l=\phi(M_{t-1}^l\mid M_{1:t-2}^l)$, and the masked tokens $\dot z_t^{l-1}=M_t^l(z_{t,1:N}^{l-1})$ contain $N'=rN$ tokens per frame. This keeps complementary spatial positions across time and facilitates local cross-frame mixing.

\paragraph{Multi-scale Channel Mixing (MCM).} Temporal dynamics are injected with a parameter-free shift trick. For a temporal scale $\delta$, channels of $\dot z_t$ are mixed with those of $\dot z_{t\pm\delta}$; let $D$ be the channel dimension and $d_\delta=\gamma D$ with hyperparameter $\gamma$. \emph{Separate mixing} gives
\begin{equation}
\dot z_{t(\delta)}=[\dot z_{t-\delta}(0{:}d_\delta);\ \dot z_{t+\delta}(d_\delta{:}2d_\delta);\ \dot z_t(2d_\delta{:}D)]\in\mathbb{R}^{N'\times D},
\end{equation}
while \emph{continual mixing} draws on a denser context; for $\delta=2$,
\begin{multline}
\dot z_{t(\delta)}=[\dot z_{t-2}(0{:}\tfrac{d_\delta}{\delta});\ \dot z_{t-1}(\tfrac{d_\delta}{\delta}{:}d_\delta);\ \dot z_{t+1}(d_\delta{:}d_\delta{+}\tfrac{d_\delta}{\delta});\\ \dot z_{t+2}(d_\delta{+}\tfrac{d_\delta}{\delta}{:}2d_\delta);\ \dot z_t(2d_\delta{:}D)].
\end{multline}
MHSA is applied to the mixed tokens, $\dot z^l_{t(\delta)}=\mathrm{MHSA}(\mathrm{LN}(\dot z^{l-1}_{t(\delta)}))+\dot z^{l-1}_{t(\delta)}$, and results for scales $\delta\in\{\pm1,\pm2,\dots,\pm S\}$ are averaged to obtain $\bar z_t^l\in\mathbb{R}^{N'\times D}$.

\paragraph{Spatial padding and shortcutting.} Since WSM reduces the tokens to $N'$, we pad back to $N$ by filling masked positions with $z'^l_t$ from the standard path and unmasked positions with $\bar z_t^l$, producing $\hat z_t^l\in\mathbb{R}^{(N+1)\times D}$. This restores the spatial layout and fuses the dynamic signal through a residual shortcut.

\paragraph{Complexity.} STCA costs $O(TN^2+S\,T\,(N')^2)=O(TN^2)$, since $N'=rN$ and $S$, $r$ are constants; it adds no attention cost over a spatial-only ViT. For comparison, AIM's factorized attention costs $O(TN^2+T^2N)$ and full space-time attention $O(T^2N^2)$.

\subsection{Spatiotemporal Text Augmentation}
\paragraph{Action Semantic Knowledge Graph.} For each category we use GPT-3.5 to build a graph capturing static objects and dynamic sub-actions. Nodes are the action, related objects and sub-actions; edges are relations (predicates) between entities, e.g., ``person--uses--rope'' and ``person--descends--vertical surface'' for abseiling. The LLM is asked for the top-$K$ objects and sub-actions ($5\le K\le10$) and concise predicates between entity pairs.

\paragraph{Prompt generation.} Spatial prompts $C^s$ are sentences from object--relation triples and emphasize static appearance and context; temporal prompts $C^t$ come from sub-action--relation triples and emphasize motion and temporal progression. The final set is $C^{st}=C^s\cup C^t$, and each prompt is encoded by the frozen CLIP text encoder into $c^{st}_{k,j}$.

\subsection{Training and Inference}
Only the vision encoder is updated. Frame-level [CLS] features $z_{n,t}$ are aligned to prompt-level text features with the fine-grained scores
\begin{align}
S^{v2t}_{n,k}&=\tfrac1T\textstyle\sum_{t=1}^{T}\max_j\, z_{n,t}^\top c^{st}_{k,j},\\
S^{t2v}_{n,k}&=\tfrac1{N^{st}}\textstyle\sum_{j=1}^{N^{st}}\max_t\, z_{n,t}^\top c^{st}_{k,j},
\end{align}
and the final score $S_{n,k}=(S^{v2t}_{n,k}+S^{t2v}_{n,k})/2$ is optimized with cross-entropy over seen classes. Feature-level distillation from frozen CLIP video features preserves generalization. At inference, zero-shot predictions are made by computing $S_{n,k}$ over the candidate classes of each test video, using 3 temporal $\times$ 1 spatial views and averaging the predictions.

\section{Experiment}
\subsection{Setup}
We train on Kinetics-400~\cite{k400} with CLIP ViT-B/16 and ViT-L/14 backbones and evaluate zero-shot on UCF101~\cite{UCF101} and HMDB51~\cite{HMDB51} under the EP1/EP2/EP3 protocols and, cross-dataset, on Kinetics-600~\cite{k600}. STCA defaults are window $2\times2$, mask ratio $r=0.5$ and temporal scales $\{\pm1,\pm2\}$. We train with 8-frame clips and test with 3 temporal $\times$ 1 spatial views. Baselines include ActionCLIP, X-CLIP, Open-VCLIP/Open-VCLIP++, FROSTER, AP-CLIP, OST, BIKE, Text4Vis and ViLT-CLIP.

\subsection{Main Results}
