\section{Limitations}
STDD depends on an LLM-generated ASKG, which may introduce noise or bias; automatic quality control or human-in-the-loop refinement could help. The hyperparameters (mask ratio $r$, window size, temporal scales $\delta$, $\gamma$) may need tuning across datasets, and our fixed temporal scales could be replaced by adaptive, content-aware selection. Extending to long-form videos and complex multi-person interactions may require hierarchical temporal modeling.

\section{Conclusion}
We presented STDD, which upgrades CLIP from a spatial to a spatiotemporal expert for ZSAR by combining parameter-free Space-time Cross Attention on the vision side with ASKG-based spatiotemporal prompts and fine-grained frame--prompt alignment on the text side. It achieves state-of-the-art results on UCF101, HMDB51 and Kinetics-600 under several protocols without increasing attention complexity.

